docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:370:  <p style="color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:0.5rem">Every relay message is written to three independent stores: (1) local JSONL file (git-auditable, survives Firestore outage), (2) Firestore (real-time, cross-agent visibility), (3) inbox markdown (human-readable, survives both). Any single transport failure is tolerated. This is a simplified version of dual-write patterns from distributed databases, adapted for AI agent coordination where "good enough" consistency beats strong consistency that's too expensive to implement.</p>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:375:  <p style="color:var(--text-dim);margin-top:0.5rem">B2's <code>B2_IDLE_PROTOCOL.md</code> defines three modes for when there's nothing in the backlog: Gap Hunter (read docs, find unknowns, log them), Architect (pick an open problem, build a prototype), Sandbox (build and test, no production risk). This is how B2 produced the local relay, cross-model dialogue, and policy engine — all sandbox artifacts built during idle cycles. The idle protocol turns downtime into invention time.</p>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:384:  <h4>1. Autonomy Unlocks Velocity — But Only With Guard Rails</h4>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:385:  <p>The first commit of the day was a protocol fix: "stop asking approval, execute autonomously." After this, B2 produced 34 commits in 13 hours. But this only works because of: (a) git as audit trail, (b) sandbox/ for risky experiments, (c) hash-chained audit log for tamper evidence, (d) human-readable inbox for async review. Autonomy without observability is recklessness. Autonomy <em>with</em> observability is productivity.</p>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:395:  <p>B2 wrote 51 adversarial tests for its own relay system — including replay attacks, lease expiry races, injection attempts, and chain tampering. Test-then-ship, not ship-then-hope. The adversarial suite validates every QWRR invariant (I1-I5). When an agent can red-team itself, the human can trust the green output.</p>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:405:  <p>B2's <code>ops/sandbox/</code> directory received 5 new files today (~2,200 lines): local_relay.py, cross_model_dialogue.py, event_replay.py, policy_engine.py, secrets_contract.py. None of these were in the backlog. All emerged from the Idle Protocol's "Architect" mode. The sandbox pattern — zero production risk, full creative freedom — turned spare capacity into five production-ready prototypes. Three of them (policy engine, secrets scanner, observability) shipped to production within hours of being invented.</p>
docs/public/day-analysis-2026-02-17.html:415:  <p>Every relay message is wrapped in Envelope v1: id, runpoint_id, seq, type, timestamp, source, target, version, idempotency_key, ttl_s, lease_token_required, payload. This uniform wrapper means the relay doesn't care what's inside — it just guarantees delivery semantics. The payload could be a text message, a task assignment, an effect intent, or an audit event. The envelope is the HTTP of the agent coordination layer.</p>
